        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Cicera',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cicera (also known as Cicera Bigia or Ciccera Biccera) is an Italian fishing card game closely related to Scopa and Scopone, played in the province of Brescia using a unique 52-card Bresciane deck. The game is the reason why Bresciane playing cards are made with 52 cards rather than the standard Italian 40 cards.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Province of Brescia, Italy\nTime Period: Historical game with long tradition\nHistorical Development: The game influenced the local card manufacturing tradition, requiring 52-card decks rather than the standard 40-card Italian packs. Related to Scopa and Scopone but with local Bresciane characteristics.\nCultural Significance: The reason Bresciane cards have 52 cards instead of 40. Similar to how Trevisane cards have 52 cards for ScarabociÃ³n.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 4\nMaximum Players: 4\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Yes, fixed partnerships (partners opposite)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Bresciane 52-card pack\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included\nCards: Re (King), Cavallo (Horse), Fante (Jack), 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, A in each suit\nAlternative: Standard international 52-card pack can be used',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Bresciane 52-card deck (or standard 52-card pack)\nScore sheet\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: 12 cards each\nTable Cards: 4 cards face-up to center\nDealing Pattern: Anticlockwise\nRedeal: If 3 or 4 Aces appear in initial table cards, reshuffle and redeal',
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
